  <user-procedures>
    <Event name="PS-LANG-CHECK" paragraph="PS-LANG-CHECK"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   The language kept in the settings; the texts follow it.

           SET     WS-SET-NAME                   TO    "LANG"

           *> PS-SETTING-GET: reads one setting from the settings file (indexed, data/settings.idx
           *> or $POWERSPATIAL_DATA/settings.idx) by its name. It never writes a setting (the OPEN
           *> only leaves an empty settings.idx.lck beside the file).
           *> Uses:   WS-SET-NAME = "LANG" (set just above; it is the record key) and the file.
           *> Result: WS-SET-VALUE = the saved language code ("pt", "es", ...), or SPACES when the
           *>         setting was never kept or the file is missing or cannot be opened.

           CALL    "PS-SETTING-GET" USING WS-SET-NAME WS-SET-VALUE

           *> WS-NEW-IX is the PS-TEXT column: 1 en, 2 pt, 3 es, 4 fr, 5 jp, 6 cn.
           *> A setting never kept, or any other code, means English.

           EVALUATE    WS-SET-VALUE(1:2)
               WHEN "pt" MOVE 2 TO WS-NEW-IX
               WHEN "es" MOVE 3 TO WS-NEW-IX
               WHEN "fr" MOVE 4 TO WS-NEW-IX
               WHEN "jp" MOVE 5 TO WS-NEW-IX
               WHEN "cn" MOVE 6 TO WS-NEW-IX
               WHEN OTHER MOVE 1 TO WS-NEW-IX
           END-EVALUATE

           *> Tmr-Lang calls this every second. WS-LANG-IX starts at 0, so the first
           *> call always applies the texts; later ones only when the language moved.

           IF    WS-NEW-IX NOT = WS-LANG-IX
               SET     WS-LANG-IX                TO    WS-NEW-IX

               *> PS-APPLY-TEXT: writes the texts of the chosen language on the dashboard.
               *> Uses:   WS-LANG-IX (just set to WS-NEW-IX, 1-6) as the PS-TEXT column; through the
               *>         redraws also WS-AC-ON, Knb-AC::Value, WS-DEV-ON(1) to (3) and the system
               *>         date.
               *> Result: Lbl-Light, Lbl-AirCon, Lbl-Wifi and Lbl-Outdoor::Caption = PS-TEXT rows 1
               *>         to 4; Txt-Search::HintText = row 5; Lbl-Hi = 6, Lbl-Album = 7, Lbl-AC = 8,
               *>         Lbl-Under = 11, Lbl-Consumption = 12, Lbl-Humidity = 13, Lbl-Pressure =
               *>         14, Lbl-Thermostat = 15, Lbl-Lighting = 16, Lbl-Speaker = 17 (all
               *>         ::Caption, trimmed). Then PS-AC-SHOW, PS-DEVICES-SHOW and PS-CLOCK redraw
               *>         what depends on a state or the date (texts, dial and switch state, device
               *>         button colours, Lbl-Date, Lbl-Time) and leave WS-N, WS-N-TXT, WS-LINE,
               *>         WS-NOW, WS-MONTH and WS-DAY changed. A changed Sw-AC::Checked raises
               *>         SW-AC--ONCHECKEDCHANGED (PS-AC-TOGGLE).

               CALL    "PS-APPLY-TEXT"
           END-IF
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-APPLY-TEXT" paragraph="PS-APPLY-TEXT"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           SET     Lbl-Light::Caption            TO    PS-TEXT(1, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-AirCon::Caption           TO    PS-TEXT(2, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Wifi::Caption             TO    PS-TEXT(3, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Outdoor::Caption          TO    PS-TEXT(4, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Txt-Search::HintText          TO    PS-TEXT(5, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Hi::Caption               TO    PS-TEXT(6, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Album::Caption            TO    PS-TEXT(7, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-AC::Caption               TO    PS-TEXT(8, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Under::Caption            TO    PS-TEXT(11, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Consumption::Caption      TO    PS-TEXT(12, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Humidity::Caption         TO    PS-TEXT(13, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Pressure::Caption         TO    PS-TEXT(14, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Thermostat::Caption       TO    PS-TEXT(15, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Lighting::Caption         TO    PS-TEXT(16, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           SET     Lbl-Speaker::Caption          TO    PS-TEXT(17, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

           *> Texts that depend on a state or on the date are set by these, not
           *> by the list above: cooling or off, device on or off, the date.

           *> PS-AC-SHOW: redraws the air conditioner card from the dial and the on/off flag: target
           *> temperature, cooling/off caption, and the state of the dial and the switch.
           *> Uses:   Knb-AC::Value (the dial, 16 to 30; 28 as designed), WS-AC-ON ("Y" at load) and
           *>         PS-TEXT rows 9 and 10 of the WS-LANG-IX column.
           *> Result: WS-N = Knb-AC::Value; WS-N-TXT = WS-N; WS-LINE = that value + "°C" (28 gives
           *>         "28°C"); Lbl-Target::Caption = WS-LINE. With WS-AC-ON = "Y":
           *>         Lbl-Cooling::Caption = PS-TEXT row 9, and Lbl-Under::Visible, Knb-AC::Enabled
           *>         and Sw-AC::Checked = 1. With "N": row 10, and the three = 0. When
           *>         Sw-AC::Checked changes the stored value it raises SW-AC--ONCHECKEDCHANGED
           *>         (PS-AC-TOGGLE), which repeats this redraw with the same values. The host
           *>         compares the text of the held and the written value, so the first write may
           *>         count as a change if its spelling differs (1 versus true): this redraw may
           *>         then run once more at load.

           CALL    "PS-AC-SHOW"

           *> PS-DEVICES-SHOW: redraws the three device cards (Thermostat, Lighting, Speaker) from
           *> their on/off flags.
           *> Uses:   WS-DEV-ON(1) to (3) ("Y" or "N"; "Y", "N", "Y" at load), PS-TEXT rows 18 and
           *>         19 of the WS-LANG-IX column.
           *> Result: for each card n that is on (WS-DEV-ON(n) = "Y"): Lbl-<card>-Status::Caption =
           *>         PS-TEXT row 18, Btn-<card>-Cast::BackgroundColor = "#0A84FFFF" (blue),
           *>         Btn-<card>-Plug::BackgroundColor = "#2DC653FF" (green). When it is off: row
           *>         19, and both buttons "#FFFFFF26" (faint white). No data item changes.

           CALL    "PS-DEVICES-SHOW"

           *> PS-CLOCK: shows the current date and time in the language on screen; called here so
           *> the date follows a language change at once, without waiting for the next Tmr-Clock
           *> tick.
           *> Uses:   FUNCTION CURRENT-DATE, WS-LANG-IX (picks the date layout) and PS-TEXT rows 21
           *>         to 32 (the month names).
           *> Result: WS-NOW = FUNCTION CURRENT-DATE; WS-MONTH, WS-DAY, WS-N-TXT and WS-LINE hold
           *>         the parts last cut from it. Lbl-Date::Caption = the date by language (shown
           *>         for 4 October 2026): en "04 October, 2026", pt "4 de outubro de 2026", es
           *>         "4 de octubre de 2026", fr "4 octobre 2026", jp/cn "2026年10月4日";
           *>         Lbl-Time::Caption = "hh:mm".

           CALL    "PS-CLOCK"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-CLOCK" paragraph="PS-CLOCK"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   The date and the time, in the operator's language.

           SET     WS-NOW                        TO    FUNCTION CURRENT-DATE

           *> CURRENT-DATE is YYYYMMDDhhmmss...: year (1:4), month (5:2), day (7:2),
           *> hour (9:2) and minute (11:2) are cut from WS-NOW by position.

           SET     WS-MONTH                      TO    WS-NOW(5:2)
           SET     WS-DAY                        TO    WS-NOW(7:2)
           SET     WS-LINE                       TO    SPACES
           SET     WS-N-TXT                      TO    WS-DAY

           *> Month names are PS-TEXT rows 21 to 32, hence WS-MONTH + 20. The day
           *> passes through WS-N-TXT (ZZ9) to drop its leading zero; the English
           *> form uses WS-NOW(7:2) and so keeps it.

           EVALUATE    WS-LANG-IX

               *>       2026年10月4日
               WHEN 5 WHEN 6
                   STRING    WS-NOW(1:4) "年" PS-TEXT(WS-MONTH + 20, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
                   WS-N-TXT::Trim() "日" INTO WS-LINE

               *>       4 de outubro de 2026 / 4 de octubre de 2026
               WHEN 2 WHEN 3
                   STRING    WS-N-TXT::Trim() " de " PS-TEXT(WS-MONTH + 20, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
                       " de " WS-NOW(1:4) INTO WS-LINE

               *>       4 octobre 2026
               WHEN 4
                   STRING    WS-N-TXT::Trim() " " PS-TEXT(WS-MONTH + 20, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
                       " " WS-NOW(1:4) INTO WS-LINE

               *>       04 October, 2026
               WHEN OTHER
                   STRING    WS-NOW(7:2) " " PS-TEXT(WS-MONTH + 20, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
                       ", " WS-NOW(1:4) INTO WS-LINE
           END-EVALUATE

           SET     Lbl-Date::Caption             TO    WS-LINE
           SET     WS-LINE                       TO    SPACES

           STRING    WS-NOW(9:2) ":" WS-NOW(11:2) INTO WS-LINE
           SET     Lbl-Time::Caption             TO    WS-LINE
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-AC-SHOW" paragraph="PS-AC-SHOW"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   The dial and its readout follow the air conditioner's switch.

           SET     WS-N                          TO    Knb-AC::Value
           SET     WS-N-TXT                      TO    WS-N
           SET     WS-LINE                       TO    SPACES

           STRING    WS-N-TXT::Trim() "°C" INTO WS-LINE
           SET     Lbl-Target::Caption           TO    WS-LINE
           IF    WS-AC-ON = "Y"
               SET     Lbl-Cooling::Caption      TO    PS-TEXT(9, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
               SET     Lbl-Under::Visible        TO    1
               SET     Knb-AC::Enabled           TO    1
               SET     Sw-AC::Checked            TO    1
           ELSE
               SET     Lbl-Cooling::Caption      TO    PS-TEXT(10, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
               SET     Lbl-Under::Visible        TO    0
               SET     Knb-AC::Enabled           TO    0
               SET     Sw-AC::Checked            TO    0
           END-IF
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-AC-TOGGLE" paragraph="PS-AC-TOGGLE"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           IF    Sw-AC::Checked = 1
               SET     WS-AC-ON                  TO    "Y"
           ELSE
               SET     WS-AC-ON                  TO    "N"
           END-IF

           *> PS-AC-SHOW: redraws the air conditioner card from the dial and the on/off flag: target
           *> temperature, cooling/off caption, and the state of the dial and the switch.
           *> Uses:   WS-AC-ON (just set from Sw-AC::Checked: "Y" when it is 1, otherwise "N"),
           *>         Knb-AC::Value and WS-LANG-IX.
           *> Result: WS-N = Knb-AC::Value, WS-N-TXT = WS-N and WS-LINE = the value + "°C" (28 gives
           *>         "28°C"); Lbl-Target::Caption = WS-LINE. With WS-AC-ON = "Y":
           *>         Lbl-Cooling::Caption = PS-TEXT row 9, Lbl-Under visible, Knb-AC enabled; with
           *>         "N": row 10, Lbl-Under hidden, Knb-AC disabled. Sw-AC::Checked is written back
           *>         with the value it already has (1 for "Y", 0 for "N"); the host raises the
           *>         switch's event only when the stored text changes.

           CALL    "PS-AC-SHOW"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-AC-TOGGLE-BUTTON" paragraph="PS-AC-TOGGLE-BUTTON"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           IF    WS-AC-ON = "Y"
               SET     WS-AC-ON                  TO    "N"
           ELSE
               SET     WS-AC-ON                  TO    "Y"
           END-IF

           *> PS-AC-SHOW: redraws the air conditioner card from the dial and the on/off flag: target
           *> temperature, cooling/off caption, and the state of the dial and the switch.
           *> Uses:   WS-AC-ON (just flipped above: "Y" became "N", or "N" became "Y"),
           *>         Knb-AC::Value and WS-LANG-IX.
           *> Result: WS-N = Knb-AC::Value, WS-N-TXT = WS-N and WS-LINE = the value + "°C" (28 gives
           *>         "28°C"); Lbl-Target::Caption = WS-LINE. Sw-AC::Checked = 1 for "Y" or 0 for
           *>         "N" (this is what moves the switch when the power button is used); when that
           *>         changes the stored value it raises SW-AC--ONCHECKEDCHANGED, which runs
           *>         PS-AC-TOGGLE and repeats this redraw with the same values. Knb-AC::Enabled and
           *>         Lbl-Under::Visible = 1 or 0 the same way; Lbl-Cooling::Caption = PS-TEXT row 9
           *>         or 10.

           CALL    "PS-AC-SHOW"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-PLAY-TOGGLE" paragraph="PS-PLAY-TOGGLE"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           IF    WS-PLAYING = "Y"
               SET     WS-PLAYING                TO    "N"
               SET     Btn-Play::IconPath        TO    "Assets/icons/play.svg"
               SET     Tmr-Wave::Enabled         TO    0
           ELSE
               SET     WS-PLAYING                TO    "Y"
               SET     Btn-Play::IconPath        TO    "Assets/icons/pause.svg"
               SET     Tmr-Wave::Enabled         TO    1
           END-IF
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-SONG-SHOW" paragraph="PS-SONG-SHOW"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           SET     Lbl-Song::Caption             TO    WS-SONG-TITLE(WS-SONG)
           SET     Lbl-Album-Name::Caption       TO    WS-SONG-ALBUM(WS-SONG)
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-SONG-NEXT" paragraph="PS-SONG-NEXT"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           IF    WS-SONG >= 3
               SET     WS-SONG                   TO    1
           ELSE
               ADD    1 TO WS-SONG
           END-IF

           *> PS-SONG-SHOW: shows the title and the album of the current song.
           *> Uses:   WS-SONG (just advanced: 1 becomes 2, 2 becomes 3, 3 wraps to 1) and the
           *>         WS-SONG-TABLE rows.
           *> Result: Lbl-Song::Caption = WS-SONG-TITLE(WS-SONG) and Lbl-Album-Name::Caption =
           *>         WS-SONG-ALBUM(WS-SONG). No data item changes.

           CALL    "PS-SONG-SHOW"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-SONG-PREV" paragraph="PS-SONG-PREV"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           IF    WS-SONG <= 1
               SET     WS-SONG                   TO    3
           ELSE
               SUBTRACT    1 FROM WS-SONG
           END-IF

           *> PS-SONG-SHOW: shows the title and the album of the current song.
           *> Uses:   WS-SONG (just moved back: 3 becomes 2, 2 becomes 1, 1 wraps to 3) and the
           *>         WS-SONG-TABLE rows.
           *> Result: Lbl-Song::Caption = WS-SONG-TITLE(WS-SONG) and Lbl-Album-Name::Caption =
           *>         WS-SONG-ALBUM(WS-SONG). No data item changes.

           CALL    "PS-SONG-SHOW"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-WAVE-INIT" paragraph="PS-WAVE-INIT"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   A waveform of 48 samples, made once.

           PERFORM    VARYING WS-I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-I > WS-WAVE-N

               *> Pseudo-random from the fixed seed in WS-SEED, so every start draws
               *> the same wave. Heights land between 15 and 99.

               COMPUTE    WS-SEED = FUNCTION MOD(WS-SEED * 73 + 41, 997)
               COMPUTE    WS-WAVE-VALUE(WS-I) = 15 + FUNCTION MOD(WS-SEED, 85)

               SET     WS-N-TXT                  TO    WS-I
               SET     WS-WAVE-LABEL(WS-I)       TO    WS-N-TXT
           END-PERFORM
           COBOL::"CHART-SET-TABLE" ( "Cht-Wave" WS-WAVE WS-WAVE-N )
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-WAVE-SHIFT" paragraph="PS-WAVE-SHIFT"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   While a song plays the waveform moves left one sample a tick.

           SET     WS-FIRST                      TO    WS-WAVE-VALUE(1)

           *> The first sample returns at the end, so the wave rotates and keeps
           *> its shape. The loop stops one short of WS-WAVE-N because it reads
           *> WS-I + 1.

           PERFORM    VARYING WS-I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-I >= WS-WAVE-N
               SET     WS-WAVE-VALUE(WS-I)       TO    WS-WAVE-VALUE(WS-I + 1)
           END-PERFORM
           SET     WS-WAVE-VALUE(WS-WAVE-N)      TO    WS-FIRST
           COBOL::"CHART-SET-TABLE" ( "Cht-Wave" WS-WAVE WS-WAVE-N )
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-DEVICES-SHOW" paragraph="PS-DEVICES-SHOW"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   Each device card shows whether the device is on.

           IF    WS-DEV-ON(1) = "Y"
               SET     Lbl-Thermostat-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(18, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               *> Same colours for all three cards (RRGGBBAA): blue cast and green plug
               *> when the device is on, a faint translucent white when it is off.

               SET     Btn-Thermostat-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#0A84FFFF"

               SET     Btn-Thermostat-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#2DC653FF"
           ELSE
               SET     Lbl-Thermostat-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(19, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               SET     Btn-Thermostat-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"

               SET     Btn-Thermostat-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"
           END-IF
           IF    WS-DEV-ON(2) = "Y"
               SET     Lbl-Lighting-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(18, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               SET     Btn-Lighting-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#0A84FFFF"

               SET     Btn-Lighting-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#2DC653FF"
           ELSE
               SET     Lbl-Lighting-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(19, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               SET     Btn-Lighting-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"

               SET     Btn-Lighting-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"
           END-IF
           IF    WS-DEV-ON(3) = "Y"
               SET     Lbl-Speaker-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(18, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               SET     Btn-Speaker-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#0A84FFFF"

               SET     Btn-Speaker-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#2DC653FF"
           ELSE
               SET     Lbl-Speaker-Status::Caption
                                                 TO    PS-TEXT(19, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()

               SET     Btn-Speaker-Cast::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"

               SET     Btn-Speaker-Plug::BackgroundColor
                                                 TO    "#FFFFFF26"
           END-IF
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-DEVICE-TOGGLE" paragraph="PS-DEVICE-TOGGLE"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   WS-I names the device card pressed.

           IF    WS-DEV-ON(WS-I) = "Y"
               SET     WS-DEV-ON(WS-I)           TO    "N"
           ELSE
               SET     WS-DEV-ON(WS-I)           TO    "Y"
           END-IF

           *> PS-DEVICES-SHOW: redraws the three device cards (Thermostat, Lighting, Speaker) from
           *> their on/off flags.
           *> Uses:   WS-DEV-ON(1) to (3), with WS-DEV-ON(WS-I) just flipped above, and PS-TEXT rows
           *>         18 and 19 of the WS-LANG-IX column.
           *> Result: for each card n that is on (WS-DEV-ON(n) = "Y"): Lbl-<card>-Status::Caption =
           *>         PS-TEXT row 18, Btn-<card>-Cast::BackgroundColor = "#0A84FFFF" (blue),
           *>         Btn-<card>-Plug::BackgroundColor = "#2DC653FF" (green). When it is off: row
           *>         19, and both buttons "#FFFFFF26" (faint white). No data item changes.

           CALL    "PS-DEVICES-SHOW"
]]></Event>
    <Event name="PS-NOTIFY" paragraph="PS-NOTIFY"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           SET     Snb-Note::Text                TO    PS-TEXT(20, WS-LANG-IX)::Trim()
           Snb-Note::Show()
]]></Event>
  </user-procedures>
  <form-events>
    <Event name="onLoad" paragraph="MAIN-FORM--ONLOAD"><![CDATA[       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.

       DATA DIVISION.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.

           *>   The dashboard opens its floating windows, docked around it.

           SET     WS-DEV-ON(1)                  TO    WS-DEV-INIT(1:1)
           SET     WS-DEV-ON(2)                  TO    WS-DEV-INIT(2:1)
           SET     WS-DEV-ON(3)                  TO    WS-DEV-INIT(3:1)

           *> WS-DEV-ON is set first: the first PS-LANG-CHECK always applies the
           *> texts (WS-LANG-IX starts at 0), and that redraws the device cards.

           *> PS-LANG-CHECK: reads the saved language and, when it is not the one on screen, applies
           *> its texts.
           *> Uses:   The LANG setting in the settings file and WS-LANG-IX = 0 (its VALUE at load).
           *>         WS-DEV-ON(1) to (3) were just set from WS-DEV-INIT ("YNY"); the redraws it
           *>         triggers also read them, WS-AC-ON, Knb-AC::Value and the system date.
           *> Result: WS-SET-NAME = "LANG"; WS-SET-VALUE = the code read (SPACES if none); WS-NEW-IX
           *>         = 1-6; WS-LANG-IX = WS-NEW-IX, so this first call always applies every caption
           *>         and the search hint (PS-APPLY-TEXT; this form sets no tooltip) and, through
           *>         it, redraws the air conditioner, the device cards and the date, which leaves
           *>         WS-N, WS-N-TXT, WS-LINE, WS-NOW, WS-MONTH and WS-DAY changed.

           CALL    "PS-LANG-CHECK"

           *> PS-WAVE-INIT: builds the waveform of 48 samples, once, and hands it to the chart.
           *> Uses:   WS-SEED = 7 (its VALUE, so every start draws the same wave) and WS-WAVE-N =
           *>         48.
           *> Result: WS-WAVE-VALUE(1) to (48) = 15 to 99, pseudo-random from WS-SEED;
           *>         WS-WAVE-LABEL(i) = i edited as ZZ9 (leading blanks). WS-SEED, WS-N-TXT and
           *>         WS-I (ends at 49) are left changed. COBOL::"CHART-SET-TABLE" hands WS-WAVE and
           *>         48 to Cht-Wave; the chart names no LabelField or ValueFields, so the built-in
           *>         reads the table as rows of X(64) + 9(18)V9(6) (88 bytes), not as these 7-byte
           *>         rows (X(4) + 9(3)): the 336 bytes make at most 3 points, misread.

           CALL    "PS-WAVE-INIT"

           *> PS-SONG-SHOW: shows the title and the album of the current song.
           *> Uses:   WS-SONG = 1 (its VALUE at load) and the WS-SONG-TABLE rows.
           *> Result: Lbl-Song::Caption = "Ed Sheeran - Shape of You" and Lbl-Album-Name::Caption =
           *>         "Divide" (row 1 of WS-SONG-TABLE). No data item changes.

           CALL    "PS-SONG-SHOW"

           INVOKE    me::OpenFormAsync("TOOLBAR-FORM")
           INVOKE    me::OpenFormAsync("RAIL-FORM")
           INVOKE    me::OpenFormAsync("ROOMS-FORM")
]]></Event>
    <Event name="onClose" paragraph="MAIN-FORM--ONCLOSE">
    </Event>
  </form-events>
